        {
          "id": "89f67d75-fed9-3d83-8e5e-91782325ab81",
          "content": {
            "id": "89f67d75-fed9-3d83-8e5e-91782325ab81",
            "contentType": "VIDEO",
            "title": "BMW boosts profit margin, Toyota raises outlook",
            "description": "<title><body><p>STORY: BMW's core profit margin rose in the third quarter.</p><p>The German automaker beat forecasts on Wednesday (November 5) with an operating margin of 5.2% for its automotive unit in the July to September period.</p><p>That was up from 2.3% a year earlier.</p><p>Group earnings before interest and tax met forecasts at $2.7 billion, while revenues were slightly weaker than expected.</p><p>BMW's results were a bright spot in another difficult quarter for Europe's carmakers, who have endured high U.S. tariffs and cheap Chinese rivals.</p><p>BMW cut its full-year guidance last month due to tariff costs and slow growth in China.</p><p>The group said it still expected the margin for cars within a range of 5% to 6%, slipping from 6.3% last year.</p><p>Wednesday's update comes as the auto giant passed the peak in research and development spending on a new all-electric range.</p><p>It expects the first model from the \"Neue Klasse\" series to drive growth next year.</p><p>Elsewhere, Japanese rival Toyota raised its full-year profit forecast Wednesday.</p><p>The automaker bet solid performance outside the U.S. would help it ride out the impact of President Donald Trump's tariffs.</p><p>The world's top-selling carmaker now expects operating profit of $22.6 billion for the financial year to the end of March.</p><p>That's up 6% from the $20.7 billion it previously forecast.</p><p>However, operating profit fell 27% to $5.4 billion in the most recent quarter - short of analyst projections.</p><p>It was Toyota's second straight decline in quarterly profits.</p><p>Shares in BMW were up around 4%, while Toyota fell just over 3.6%.</p></body></title>",
            "summary": "<body><p>STORY: BMW's core profit margin rose in the third quarter.</p><p>The German automaker beat forecasts on Wednesday (November 5) with an operating margin of 5.2% for its automotive unit in the July to September period.</p><p>That was up from 2.3% a year earlier.</p><p>Group earnings before interest and tax met forecasts at $2.7 billion, while revenues were slightly weaker than expected.</p><p>BMW's results were a bright spot in another difficult quarter for Europe's carmakers, who have endured high U.S. tariffs and cheap Chinese rivals.</p><p>BMW cut its full-year guidance last month due to tariff costs and slow growth in China.</p><p>The group said it still expected the margin for cars within a range of 5% to 6%, slipping from 6.3% last year.</p><p>Wednesday's update comes as the auto giant passed the peak in research and development spending on a new all-electric range.</p><p>It expects the first model from the \"Neue Klasse\" series to drive growth next year.</p><p>Elsewhere, Japanese rival Toyota raised its full-year profit forecast Wednesday.</p><p>The automaker bet solid performance outside the U.S. would help it ride out the impact of President Donald Trump's tariffs.</p><p>The world's top-selling carmaker now expects operating profit of $22.6 billion for the financial year to the end of March.</p><p>That's up 6% from the $20.7 billion it previously forecast.</p><p>However, operating profit fell 27% to $5.4 billion in the most recent quarter - short of analyst projections.</p><p>It was Toyota's second straight decline in quarterly profits.</p><p>Shares in BMW were up around 4%, while Toyota fell just over 3.6%.</p></body>",
